852" :question/math-academy-id "q-50148" :question/problem "Solve the differential equation\n$\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} - e^{3x} = 0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097308 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "23789a74-f1e3-5e26-8678-21f84a11a12b" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Solution Curves of a Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09e8c57d-66b6-54a2-a409-f04037ac2dd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/8b/8bd666d1415f83ff8232e39963248ef0afe62f39e66d5f3416ae18316453faa3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392716} {:answer/id #uuid "4a7c9c8c-a18a-575b-bbc5-e03ae2420c9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/5d/5d686380cd025e83e38524918c5cfaf215726f0f0d05783b8f88fd5f281c091c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392717} {:answer/id #uuid "f007b1ae-80cd-5a65-860b-3cf4ed12c156" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/0a/0ab0b12088ac6816f7086e952e749ea6d4c9107cc6189ebb0efcafb904152ad0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392718} {:answer/id #uuid "c7ff690e-b86c-57a2-9518-abe046479e48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/65/65ae1770ff4084d5a89fdda0f552f2a92380ae948c2b0f1ef3d3bb5d20f32713.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392719} {:answer/id #uuid "06d4b073-7af6-56cc-939c-5a4f1e989fae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1a/1a0e6d6d025e2f8b79019036637efd78b89954d1b01b00037ece77df4c3844eb.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392720}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f007b1ae-80cd-5a65-860b-3cf4ed12c156" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/0a/0ab0b12088ac6816f7086e952e749ea6d4c9107cc6189ebb0efcafb904152ad0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392718} :answer-field/id #uuid "de7f2c5a-e3a6-5cfd-9077-9d13a03ddc70" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392715}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a33d5262-3afa-580b-b512-aba7c2576e69" :question/math-academy-id "q-50195" :question/problem "Which of the following shows some of the solution curves of the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2(x-1)?$" :question/worked-solution "Integrating the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int (x-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =2\\int (x-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(x-1)^{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plot the solution curves by selecting different values for $C.$ The sketch below shows the solution curves for $C=-1,0,1.$\n\n![](images/92/92a2dbaa5726e6a1ab83fa879ad7b630b05e68a8e366db214b74d85269a849a9.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097311 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9688df89-056e-59d7-869e-9841715b2aea" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Antiderivative of a Vector-Valued Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80530319-a6b8-55b9-b731-4ad7a4a59618" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- ⟨e^{t} - 4t, \\cos t⟩ + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095761} {:answer/id #uuid "e72a7913-4e20-5a4f-a654-a1c67970257e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨e^{t},-\\cos t⟩ + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095762} {:answer/id #uuid "46a55a84-cf37-5290-a61f-d84df6936650" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨e^{t} - 4t,-\\cos t⟩ + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095763} {:answer/id #uuid "54d4ed94-c606-5048-9de8-699236ff79a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨e^{t} - 4t, \\sin t⟩ + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095764} {:answer/id #uuid "599e1a14-9e35-5198-93b5-4a66b496dbe8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨e^{t} - 4t, \\cos t⟩ + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095765}] :answer-field/id #uuid "48f6bbb6-84b4-5734-8cf4-b84cb1b06c3e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095760}] :question/id #uuid "38244b69-b2ff-532d-ba91-ffcf585c5c43" :question/math-academy-id "q-50202" :question/problem "If $F^{′}(t) = ⟨e^{t} - 4, \\sin t⟩$, then $F(t) =$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392721 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d2a41ede-381e-55f6-99e5-1baf1ea348a1" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Integral of a Trigonometric Expression Using Integration by Parts Twice"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e989ff47-d410-5b01-a943-55fae4916d7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{2}{3}\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392723} {:answer/id #uuid "d2815ebf-4757-56c9-91cd-cb0eb91dbf32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392724} {:answer/id #uuid "41cb0d63-902e-524a-bf02-6e3c4af5fca2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{2}+\\frac{3}{2}\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392725} {:answer/id #uuid "92c3b147-4088-52d5-b2f3-b4ece5e73d28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{2}{3}\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392726} {:answer/id #uuid "e77630a7-5ba0-527b-b582-20214c8e5890" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392727}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e989ff47-d410-5b01-a943-55fae4916d7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{2}{3}\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392723} :answer-field/id #uuid "b56c8992-3610-5b4f-9996-cd46ce9731ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392722}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f83185ca-7204-529d-8697-1b2d513b1e26" :question/math-academy-id "q-50222" :question/problem "$\\int {x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "We need to use integration by parts. \n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={x}^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({x}^{2})(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3})-\\int (-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3})(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{2}{3}\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392728 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "072ad553-dd0b-58e0-b3e2-082c6d9866dd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Vector-Valued Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9fde686e-0040-50c8-9b9b-1ad4f1e8aaa2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{{e}^{2t}}{{({e}^{t}+1)}^{2}},\\,5{t}^{4}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392730} {:answer/id #uuid "d02ac812-19ad-5839-9ad9-16c2a59beade" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{{e}^{t}}{{({e}^{t}+1)}^{2}},\\,5{t}^{4}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392731} {:answer/id #uuid "bc4ffd3e-cd2a-5031-83aa-72de86237fff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{{e}^{2t}}{{({e}^{t}+1)}^{2}},\\,5t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392732} {:answer/id #uuid "dd140ea3-6577-5157-ab09-4fefef73b8e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{2{e}^{2t}-{e}^{t}}{{({e}^{t}+1)}^{2}},\\,{t}^{4}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392733} {:answer/id #uuid "37a5973e-637b-5d29-9329-f2c326b69242" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{{e}^{t}}{{({e}^{t}+1)}^{2}},\\,5t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392734}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d02ac812-19ad-5839-9ad9-16c2a59beade" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{{e}^{t}}{{({e}^{t}+1)}^{2}},\\,5{t}^{4}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392731} :answer-field/id #uuid "03ef94ed-b793-5302-a468-c42ecf1df591" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392729}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "607151f7-a0f9-522f-b6fe-540456f7af54" :question/math-academy-id "q-50371" :question/problem "Given that $r(t)=⟨\\frac{{e}^{t}}{{e}^{t}+1},\\,{t}^{5}⟩,$ ${r}^{′}(t)=$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate each component separately.\n\n- For the $x$-component $x(t)=\\frac{{e}^{t}}{{e}^{t}+1},$ we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{{e}^{t}({e}^{t}+1)-{e}^{t}\\cdot {e}^{t}}{{({e}^{t}+1)}^{2}}=\\frac{{e}^{t}}{{({e}^{t}+1)}^{2}}.\n$$\n\n- For the $y$-component $y(t)={t}^{5},$ we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(t)=5{t}^{4}.\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨{x}^{′}(t),\\,{y}^{′}(t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{{e}^{t}}{{({e}^{t}+1)}^{2}},\\,5{t}^{4}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392735 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f0e2596-73d6-540c-af84-b19a943da6a8" :knowledge-point/title "The Second Derivative of a Vector-Valued Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fe19103f-5f99-576d-a025-053904208525" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2\\operatorname{sec}⁡t\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡t({tan}^{2}⁡t+{sec}^{2}⁡t)⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392737} {:answer/id #uuid "e01882fe-f747-5560-b961-3aeaa74d08c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2{sec}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,{sec}^{3}⁡t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392738} {:answer/id #uuid "574da43a-a87f-5e11-8117-2e74261fac00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2{sec}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡t({tan}^{2}⁡t+{sec}^{2}⁡t)⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392739} {:answer/id #uuid "8eccfb9d-3d94-5276-98aa-105bb31f0184" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2{sec}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡t({tan}^{2}⁡t+\\operatorname{sec}⁡t)⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392740} {:answer/id #uuid "13350f1d-caa5-59e1-8249-6fc0fdc305b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2\\operatorname{sec}⁡t\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,{sec}^{3}⁡t\\operatorname{tan}⁡t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392741}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "574da43a-a87f-5e11-8117-2e74261fac00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2{sec}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡t({tan}^{2}⁡t+{sec}^{2}⁡t)⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392739} :answer-field/id #uuid "30603a02-99ff-59c6-861c-ce870828f5cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392736}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7fd0a18c-43f2-5761-ad4b-96e6a87257fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-50467" :question/problem "Given that $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡t⟩,$ ${r}^{″}(t)=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the second derivative of a vector-valued function, we find the second derivatives of the components.\n\n- For $x(t)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & =2{sec}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For $y(t)=\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡t\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ {y}^{″}(t) & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡t{tan}^{2}⁡t+{sec}^{3}⁡t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡t({tan}^{2}⁡t+{sec}^{2}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨{x}^{″}(t),\\,{y}^{″}(t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2{sec}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡t({tan}^{2}⁡t+{sec}^{2}⁡t)⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064894 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fa1ddb40-3494-541c-b23e-b15dd98bf669" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Reason for a Discontinuity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ae63895f-26a5-550a-9e82-7414623e5158" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(-1)$ does not exist" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063391} {:answer/id #uuid "df6a43b6-1b71-5020-aa36-4c0f562445ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "lim_(x → - 1^{-})f(x) \\ne lim_(x → - 1^{+})f(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063392} {:answer/id #uuid "19140e5f-24a2-5763-ba7e-b7d994f1db7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "lim_(x → - 1)f(x) \\ne f(-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063393} {:answer/id #uuid "2403be5a-750d-57cf-99a8-51653ca8fe77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)$ is a piecewise function" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063394} {:answer/id #uuid "32cdefd9-4378-561b-a0cd-f3b8e222c2a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$lim_(x → - 1^{+})f(x)$ does not exist" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063395}] :answer-field/id #uuid "07a26449-9faf-5a21-a528-5c30e4b24664" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063390}] :question/id #uuid "ca98ec7d-0763-558c-b634-f1da93c05079" :question/math-academy-id "q-51054" :question/problem "For what reason does the function $f(x)$ below have a discontinuity at $x =-1$?\n$f(x) = {x^{3} + x,\\begin{vmatrix}x < - 1 \\\\ x^{2} + 1,\\end{vmatrix}x \\ge - 1$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064896 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "51c15d7a-cd9c-58df-a750-97df0b695c64" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Intervals of Concavity From the Graph of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9785058c-329b-55fc-9637-7ceee865761c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-3, 1)$ and $(1, 3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063403} {:answer/id #uuid "aded52a6-93b0-511f-9f1b-d35eef943b7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(3, 5)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063404} {:answer/id #uuid "e1d58dbe-fb8b-5092-b964-5ef385a425a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1, 3)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063405} {:answer/id #uuid "ec9fa222-9117-541a-9cf6-4897b1674212" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-3, 1)$ and $(3, 5)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063406} {:answer/id #uuid "57a776a1-07fd-5790-8507-49d58efecc56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1, 3)$ and $(1, 5)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063407}] :answer-field/id #uuid "2597a55f-1687-5449-83d5-f62940cb9a48" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063402}] :question/id #uuid "dd3f3945-1d74-5b64-9e26-f19b51b6c5b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-51250" :question/problem "![](images/23/237c36808ded541ed264826866db3d2774dc2d5212f1292f21a6c25b3b07d0e1.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y = f(x)$, defined on $[- 3, 5]$. Find the intervals on which the function $f(x)$ is concave down."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083778 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "965026e6-db46-5930-8844-c423e6ea795a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral Representation For an Arc Length"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f291e03-abb0-5fe4-a583-4a48393d5666" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-3}^{4}\\sqrt{16{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392743} {:answer/id #uuid "83980ef2-3253-5c8c-b7ac-9bf91e820d7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-3}^{4}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392744} {:answer/id #uuid "16e21676-9e8e-5cda-891a-9e3a99ad370a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-3}^{4}\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392745} {:answer/id #uuid "c65e70ed-792a-59d7-8500-3fca29572bba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-3}^{4}\\sqrt{{x}^{4}+1}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392746} {:answer/id #uuid "301b975b-e18b-51b6-9517-6f4e286dee3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-3}^{4}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392747}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "301b975b-e18b-51b6-9517-6f4e286dee3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-3}^{4}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392747} :answer-field/id #uuid "aca7ae0e-ab85-5150-9d28-8959d835e214" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392742}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b2c7c9a6-db2e-5889-8918-df15ae7908cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-51267" :question/problem "![](images/bb/bb30925ffaca0ce59de7508e0f3710fbb26f3a80dfbd80b689e9ee5a4cf7d699.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the length of the curve $y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}$ for $x\\in [-3,4],$ as shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4},$ $a=-3,$ and $b=4.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{2x}{4}=\\frac{x}{2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{x}{2}$ into the arc length formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{-3}^{4}\\sqrt{1+{[{f}^{′}(x)]}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{4}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-3}^{4}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079067 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "697f9bd8-9edf-5643-8cdb-a821b6dce09c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Center and Radius of a Circle by Completing the Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a6ffeffb-ef59-54f5-be3f-a2062b83999e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(2,-3)$, radius: $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077151} {:answer/id #uuid "a8e22452-4b20-5bf4-9090-9c4baa1361ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(3,-2)$, radius: $16$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077152} {:answer/id #uuid "c8f09b0a-5fff-5884-866a-7cd7c2b038b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(3,-2)$, radius: $8$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077153} {:answer/id #uuid "d6f5be55-2167-551f-a536-04e193c35eb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(-2, 3)$, radius: $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077154} {:answer/id #uuid "00169a7a-33bd-5ce6-8aa9-6f23a52c4730" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(2,-3)$, radius: $16$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077155}] :answer-field/id #uuid "b0de6a19-461b-5d8f-8397-c1cfef2f142b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077150}] :question/id #uuid "dd6c82b9-14ab-538d-aa0f-037306546c3d" :question/math-academy-id "q-5144" :question/problem "Find the center and radius of the circle whose equation is $x^{2} + y^{2} + 4x - 6y = 3$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392748 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6a76efbd-cefd-5aa0-953e-8ab50bec7ae0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Height Corresponding to an Extended Base"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c69a7c27-09ce-5cf4-9117-95fab7c198f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d5/d525b53f78cef4edb905e328ae77913f49860ca2dc2c6e1fac9db76873ac7d2a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392750} {:answer/id #uuid "d0db377a-eee0-547b-a96b-5bf2faaeab19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/46/46b12df6f2c0d61cc7296921fb335337878249ff2fe16cbab87f0048c4abb538.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392751} {:answer/id #uuid "05352607-bc7f-52c9-b024-45731a62e3a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c8/c8ed829c8e900ee2849d49202daedfe5c0978e5d6991fcce4bfe6c609aca3fb2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392752} {:answer/id #uuid "9842c750-4db7-5d95-8ed5-7083875ea3a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ae/ae60cff8e3670c2462ca1cf67179f6534f091c2f8bbbe4e1684141d78516e138.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392753} {:answer/id #uuid "38a9fbb8-74f8-5733-8270-a1e4e8bc058a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/df/df918f525274479ddf12fd1940408be116c92dbe93ca19e46676ec07dc5121a8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392754}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "38a9fbb8-74f8-5733-8270-a1e4e8bc058a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/df/df918f525274479ddf12fd1940408be116c92dbe93ca19e46676ec07dc5121a8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392754} :answer-field/id #uuid "57ee0022-49d2-518a-8af8-3f5a4819dda7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392749}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ae5e3c63-85f2-5299-87ad-9a88584d86eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-52943" :question/problem "![](images/f6/f6af66b53757930bc053ddc814583710ab0f625f343d979e7d46396d6905a6ef.png)\n\nGiven $△ABC$ shown above, which of the following diagrams represents the height corresponding to $\\overset{―}{AC}?$" :question/worked-solution "The height (or altitude) corresponding to $\\overset{―}{AC}$ is a line segment that \n\n- runs from the opposite vertex $B$ to the line $\\overset{⟷}{AC},$ and\n\n- is perpendicular to $\\overset{⟷}{AC}.$\n\nTo draw the height, we proceed as follows:\n\n- Extend the line segment $\\overset{―}{AC}.$\n\n![](images/5e/5ebb72b8b79beefebac6ebd4a34cf229bc644d48a8de711eda1f2e20c31a67ec.png)\n\n- Draw the perpendicular from $B$ to $\\overset{⟷}{AC}.$\n\n![](images/df/df918f525274479ddf12fd1940408be116c92dbe93ca19e46676ec07dc5121a8.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392755 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b6c58537-344e-5842-b68c-d9b5a9d7ca96" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Area of a Triangle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fca47e6f-7077-58c5-9eba-fabbd0424cfa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392757} {:answer/id #uuid "fe94b7cf-d6f0-5d8d-967d-01cf37ded656" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392758} {:answer/id #uuid "e86614c0-7a63-506e-b785-467d7b3a9afa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "35\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392759} {:answer/id #uuid "b98274de-d364-5518-bde6-2ee6f9eaaa05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392760} {:answer/id #uuid "aa036e36-7f2f-52d3-9e3f-bd2def22e6b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392761}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b98274de-d364-5518-bde6-2ee6f9eaaa05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392760} :answer-field/id #uuid "2dd95d01-f696-54ec-8302-29c76fdbdf3c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392756}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "823b1b37-db73-5eaa-b884-1ecc4dfb7bdf" :question/math-academy-id "q-53154" :question/problem "![](images/40/40a654166838ab16d5dc300e3c0eab894a6754a47edc68051336f4ea2a5db2e8.png)\n\nWhat is the area of the triangle $△ABC$ shown above?\n\n(Figure not drawn to scale)" :question/worked-solution "The area of the triangle is defined as \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{b\\cdot h}{2},\n$$\n\n where $b$ is a base and $h$ is the corresponding height.\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the base is \n\n$$\nb=AB=5\\,\\text{m}\n$$\n\nand the corresponding height is \n\n$$\nh=12\\,\\text{m}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{b\\cdot h}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\cdot 12}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{60}{2} \\\\  & =30\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392762 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d89ba41-6d24-548a-bd97-66830b752ba9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Total Area Bounded By a Curve When It Crosses the X-Axis Several Times"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cfb87908-0820-5ed9-9c74-e694fd902ed0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{0}-({x}^{5}-x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{5}-x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392764} {:answer/id #uuid "2601aadd-9cbb-5cc4-9558-fb7e3852b604" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{0}({x}^{5}-x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}-({x}^{5}-x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392765} {:answer/id #uuid "42b42b06-1459-571f-85f4-60f215cb6f4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{5}-x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392766} {:answer/id #uuid "e10d1563-93c7-515c-ac59-8784534bd860" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{0}({x}^{5}-x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{2}-({x}^{5}-x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392767} {:answer/id #uuid "fe6c2771-7902-5c0c-83a0-d6defda501dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{2}({x}^{5}-x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392768}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2601aadd-9cbb-5cc4-9558-fb7e3852b604" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{0}({x}^{5}-x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}-({x}^{5}-x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392765} :answer-field/id #uuid "0392da4a-7c40-5083-8000-8d46e792fc8e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392763}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "684f1c1d-407b-5f72-a75f-8a2754804ac2" :question/math-academy-id "q-54522" :question/problem "![](images/7f/7ffd75051b71a3b8665ac68418ff24dac2124a6b7ff50633a0a5843df9cff196.png)\n\nWhich integral expression gives the total area enclosed between the curve $y={x}^{5}-x$ and the $x$-axis, as shown above?" :question/worked-solution "First, we the limits of integration. We find the $x$-intercepts of the curve as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{5}-x & =0 \\\\ x({x}^{4}-1) & =0 \\\\ x({x}^{2}-1)({x}^{2}+1) & =0 \\\\ x({x}^{2}-1) & =0 \\\\ x(x-1)(x+1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $x=\\pm 1$ or $x=0.$ \nThis gives us two regions: \n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-1$ to $x=0.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=0$ to $x=1.$\n\nFor $x\\in [-1,0]$, we have\n\n$$\n{A}_{1}={\\int }_{-1}^{0}({x}^{5}-x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNotice that for $x\\in [0,1]$ the region lies completely below the $x$-axis. Hence, the value of\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{5}-x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwill be negative. Therefore, the area that we want is given by\n\n$$\n{A}_{2}=-{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{5}-x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{0}^{1}-({x}^{5}-x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFinally, the total area is\n\n$$\nA={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}={\\int }_{-1}^{0}({x}^{5}-x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}-({x}^{5}-x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392769 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "256d3a08-0ade-51e9-887b-67250d5fae21" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Number of Permutations of Objects Chosen From a Group of at Least Ten"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c0f272ea-eaec-5ac0-9b1d-aed0b3b9e6ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "78\\,960\\,960" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392771} {:answer/id #uuid "c41d4f0f-8496-5262-aa00-f1d571ba31b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "780\\,560" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392772} {:answer/id #uuid "871096f9-d17a-5038-8600-80bf7c8a9394" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120\\,450" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392773} {:answer/id #uuid "e4e17fa3-d32f-56b3-bdab-0e009c6576c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24\\,670\\,820" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392774} {:answer/id #uuid "e3e46837-c2dd-594c-b9df-46f81facc364" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,320\\,560" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392775}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c0f272ea-eaec-5ac0-9b1d-aed0b3b9e6ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "78\\,960\\,960" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392771} :answer-field/id #uuid "82710d4a-5d29-5135-9672-3c9a534fb117" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392770}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9b6ce883-6a1c-54f4-85a1-b5c70b81dd2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-5570" :question/problem "Cheryl holds a bag of $40$ marbles, each one with a slightly different size or color. If she picks $5$ of them out one at a time and lines up in a row, how many different lines of $5$ marbles are possible?" :question/worked-solution "The number of permutations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{P}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!}.\n$$\n\nThere are $40$ marbles to choose from, so $n=40.$ We want to select $5$ of those marbles for the line, so we have $r=5.$ \n\nTherefore, the number of different ways to line up $5$ marbles is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{40}{P}_{5} & =\\frac{40!}{(40-5)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{40!}{35!} \\\\  & =\\frac{40\\cdot 39\\cdot 38\\cdot 37\\cdot 36\\cdot \\cancel{35!}}{\\cancel{35!}} \\\\  & =40\\cdot 39\\cdot 38\\cdot 37\\cdot 36 \\\\  & =78\\,960\\,960.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392776 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d22726fc-8e6b-5e22-80c8-fbc2aa245217" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Number of Combinations of Objects Chosen From a Group of at Least Ten"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f089b840-e005-5e7a-9ca8-910c63c69a44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,003" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392778} {:answer/id #uuid "4fd33d27-4e9f-5373-9545-90720050f17a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "920" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392779} {:answer/id #uuid "15326776-61b2-52db-9fb8-82d245b1e023" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "545" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392780} {:answer/id #uuid "21f6aac5-0325-5ba0-86e1-61b997bc4914" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,747" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392781} {:answer/id #uuid "469416b3-6dc5-5ab8-a00f-8cc01934b7c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36\\,045" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392782}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f089b840-e005-5e7a-9ca8-910c63c69a44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,003" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392778} :answer-field/id #uuid "9798efc4-d9be-59d0-aea4-2381274e684a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392777}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0f0b6262-099b-5e2a-971d-80d2d850edfd" :question/math-academy-id "q-5571" :question/problem "A teacher gives you a test with a set of fifteen questions, out of which you are told to answer ten. How many ways can you answer ten different questions?" :question/worked-solution "In general, the number of combinations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{C}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!\\,r!}.\n$$\n\nThere are $15$ questions, so $n=15.$ We want to select $10$ of those questions to answer, so we have $r=10.$\n\nTherefore, the number of ways to answer $10$ different questions is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{15}{C}_{10} & =\\frac{15!}{(15-10)!\\,10!} \\\\  & =\\frac{15!}{5!10!} \\\\  & =\\frac{15\\cdot 14\\cdot 13\\cdot 12\\cdot 11\\cdot \\cancel{10!}}{5!\\cancel{10!}} \\\\  & =\\frac{15\\cdot 14\\cdot 13\\cdot 12\\cdot 11}{5\\cdot 4\\cdot 3\\cdot 2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{360\\,360}{120} \\\\  & =3\\,003.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392783 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "88580aff-e043-58ea-a8ca-1ce32026428f" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Speed-Time Graph for a Real-Life Scenario"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f8831562-bcba-5d5e-86bd-bd2156c1e48a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/7c/7c3e6680c0a158181e11b473a94fa43b1a2afb3ea5c6206244801f4830bafff8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392785} {:answer/id #uuid "8e0a8238-9f58-50ad-9590-85298b9c467a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/11/11691073c02c80a9c4400623a8ee648d8a9405faab89ac8ce4fdbb7dbdd61baa.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392786} {:answer/id #uuid "76325543-0212-5f9d-8b9d-4de0bdfd65f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d1/d1123b8a9a86288380e084f8001ef5ebcc160d5647c83f3878a3d7b357087a9a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392787} {:answer/id #uuid "6258ba45-43e7-5c96-b19f-b0486d5d81c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ec/ec8ff99395b5f33f531303acd91c58ada3b7f71213a5af5c76c86dae62d277e9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392788} {:answer/id #uuid "19c2e144-25c8-5914-9f81-2dab848d8fc1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/fa/fae4c7d8cfc79dd70041ebd697226408a5cdd0676ad2ddfaad1500ea1ba0a76e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392789}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19c2e144-25c8-5914-9f81-2dab848d8fc1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/fa/fae4c7d8cfc79dd70041ebd697226408a5cdd0676ad2ddfaad1500ea1ba0a76e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392789} :answer-field/id #uuid "238dbbfc-eaba-5eb7-9c11-94c48e0f1e4d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392784}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3482860b-89cd-5fe9-aa14-3b6ba9d6957f" :question/math-academy-id "q-59760" :question/problem "A particle accelerates from rest with a constant acceleration of $5\\,{\\text{km/h}}^{2}$ for the first $4$ hours of its motion. It then travels at a constant speed for the next $6$ hours. Which graph represents the described motion?" :question/worked-solution "We start the first line from the point $(0,0),$ since the particle starts from rest. Then, because the acceleration is $5\\,{\\text{km/h}}^{2},$ we draw a line with slope $5.$ \n\nHowever, because the particle is only accelerating for the first $4$ hours, we stop the line at $t=4.$ At this time, the particle's speed has increased by $4\\cdot 5=20\\text{ km/h}.$ So, the first line segment goes from $(0,0)$ to $(4,20).$\n\n![](images/cf/cf0471f38486963f4237b4fab7c1cd109d02cc2d7ac3e30990831654f04c5b11.png)\n\nThe particle then travels at a constant speed for the next $6$ hours. So, the speed remains at $20\\,\\text{km/h}$ for the next $6$ hours, until $t=4+6=10.$\n\nSo, the second line segment stops at the point $(10,20).$ The final graph is\n\n![](images/fa/fae4c7d8cfc79dd70041ebd697226408a5cdd0676ad2ddfaad1500ea1ba0a76e.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392790 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "965026e6-db46-5930-8844-c423e6ea795a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral Representation For an Arc Length"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "130902b7-518f-5bff-b4c6-70ec10669ed6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\sqrt{{ln}^{2}⁡x+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+2}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392792} {:answer/id #uuid "2e9dc4bc-d4d3-5b8e-abbf-e99a3aff279c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\sqrt{2{ln}^{2}⁡x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+2}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392793} {:answer/id #uuid "c337a131-ba2b-58de-bb8a-9bc3f5b91c72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+2}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392794} {:answer/id #uuid "104a11b9-60b8-543e-98fa-97a1a9ca9dd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\sqrt{2{ln}^{2}⁡x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392795} {:answer/id #uuid "c97ec70f-712d-5261-a2ad-b13ddf434c3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392796}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "130902b7-518f-5bff-b4c6-70ec10669ed6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\sqrt{{ln}^{2}⁡x+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+2}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392792} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8ab54e5-e3ae-5c28-96df-43d45b975ac9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392791}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1018367e-83ad-5af3-99ec-2abf57016cee" :question/math-academy-id "q-6001" :question/problem "The length of the curve $y=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ on $[1,e]$ is given by" :question/worked-solution "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ $a=1,$ and $b=e.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${f}^{′}(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1$ into the arc length formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\sqrt{1+{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\sqrt{{ln}^{2}⁡x+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184821 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "257b907c-70c1-5bbd-a6d2-297d51325d1f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation With a Positive Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82072ee0-13f5-5686-82b1-ae2000b3312f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q =-1, x = 1 ± \\sqrt{\\frac{11}{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183506} {:answer/id #uuid "92fce56f-5163-5f74-bfab-b2b7fd956c29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q =-1, x = 1 ± \\sqrt{22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183507} {:answer/id #uuid "c70de482-14f4-5806-bb6e-7e2b7620519b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q = 11, x =-1 ± \\sqrt{\\frac{11}{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183508} {:answer/id #uuid "0f68b0e9-0392-527a-bc03-1df0c43ae5f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q = 11, x = 1 ± \\sqrt{\\frac{11}{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183509} {:answer/id #uuid "3a33f08d-733e-5fc5-a89e-856cabe406e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q = 11, x = 1 ± \\sqrt{22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183510}] :answer-field/id #uuid "9790c489-8408-5721-a9a9-6746e95691ec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183505}] :question/id #uuid "404aac0a-c4f3-5720-8a82-6f8114d49cae" :question/math-academy-id "q-60360" :question/problem "The equation $2x^{2} - 4x - 9 = 0$ can be written in the form $2(x + p)^{2} = q$. Find $q$, and solve the equation."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392797 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "947eb2bb-0443-5740-9d45-847c70877270" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Surface Area of a Polyhedron Given Some Lengths"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fd2cc1f4-4180-5121-81ee-6ce90dc3ab03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "112\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392799} {:answer/id #uuid "c0f0316f-1f80-5555-873b-bb68be412b12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "80\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392800} {:answer/id #uuid "b093fa83-8105-5eda-a415-434ce5770775" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392801} {:answer/id #uuid "61e1f5e1-0d03-55f3-aeeb-7124734a0c8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "128\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392802} {:answer/id #uuid "9edb4e16-4920-586e-a93a-7fa6823134a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "96\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392803}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9edb4e16-4920-586e-a93a-7fa6823134a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "96\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392803} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f2893cb-ce4a-55a0-b185-2a569f1674bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392798}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f6441de6-8de3-5bac-a175-59bb496af580" :question/math-academy-id "q-61906" :question/problem "![](images/58/586b2c9d3409b68436f16715937b7a4bf0abdf8c1a05c628ccafdc49cf6d641d.png)\n\nWhat is the total surface area of the cube above if it is $4\\,\\text{cm}$ long, $4\\,\\text{cm}$ wide, and $4\\,\\text{cm}$ tall?" :question/worked-solution "First, let's consider the net of our plank.\n\n![](images/2c/2c8a118f8f3c3ccba67e25ae09096ef1ec0cf15fc6a5e7f9de68f797b7105c80.png)\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, we have squares only.\n\nThe squares are $4\\,\\text{cm}$ long and $4\\,\\text{cm}$ high. So, the area of one square is:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area of the square}=4\\times 4=16\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the surface area of the cube consists of $6$ squares. \n\nTherefore, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Surface area} & =6\\times (\\text{Area of the square}) \\\\  & =6\\times 16 \\\\  & =96\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392804 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "947eb2bb-0443-5740-9d45-847c70877270" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Surface Area of a Polyhedron Given Some Lengths"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d7c7996b-19b7-5eb7-84a5-17cf5d9e6167" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "125\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392806} {:answer/id #uuid "edcc69bf-98aa-5035-96a7-755c3f2d413b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "175\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392807} {:answer/id #uuid "cf607e4d-7a99-5fe8-8f93-6dbd45209478" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "200\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392808} {:answer/id #uuid "1e1baad8-edab-507a-9ec5-c266a0e0dc81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "150\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392809} {:answer/id #uuid "079063e4-8fc3-5c8e-919a-c94bf423e9d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "100\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392810}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e1baad8-edab-507a-9ec5-c266a0e0dc81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "150\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392809} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2c09b90-906a-56bc-b30d-a59eaa276027" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392805}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e80ccdcb-f8a7-5672-8914-73b42c8ccce6" :question/math-academy-id "q-61907" :question/problem "![](images/d4/d480892817efecf5ebea6860bece7cbae8127a9840e94ece34f9e83313abd5e5.png)\n\nWhat is the total surface area of the cube above if it is $5\\,\\text{cm}$ long, $5\\,\\text{cm}$ wide, and $5\\,\\text{cm}$ tall?" :question/worked-solution "First, let's consider the net of our plank.\n\n![](images/ef/efca24e18b188363ec3698ff4deaa5d5bd4ec1bdb5075138f925573a407c1f79.png)\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, we have squares only.\n\nThe squares are $5\\,\\text{cm}$ long and $5\\,\\text{cm}$ high. So, the area of one square is:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area of the square}=5\\times 5=25\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the surface area of the cube consists of $6$ squares. \n\nTherefore, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Surface area} & =6\\times (\\text{Area of the square}) \\\\  & =6\\times 25 \\\\  & =150\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147959 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1233be4b-7634-5882-9ae5-3200a9da71d8" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Surface Area of a Polyhedron Given the Areas of the Faces: Word Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c62a915-4276-57db-8a63-27f35610e126" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392812} {:answer/id #uuid "0bfef42c-d56a-5722-b2d8-f7b49fa0bfcd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392813} {:answer/id #uuid "fbadcc5d-ef99-5cdf-90c9-7093b52691c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392814} {:answer/id #uuid "276a0529-d987-5db6-83be-2028c8e08e75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392815} {:answer/id #uuid "36fd2e7a-ea93-51ed-a558-c240ada5c964" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392816}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "276a0529-d987-5db6-83be-2028c8e08e75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392815} :answer-field/id #uuid "4bd9e8a6-49c6-5c2b-8193-1c3fddac210a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392811}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "afc6c730-43c5-56ca-913e-065a04d0dcda" :question/math-academy-id "q-62051" :question/problem "![](images/9e/9e36e33a8d00ff486762e0eafad6e1420fab27f8721c7bdd1d1d6c6062cd1332.png)\n\nDave unfolds a cardboard box and gets the net shown above. In the figure, the areas of two of its faces are shown. What is the total surface area of the box?" :question/worked-solution "The net shown corresponds to a rectangular prism. The surface area of our prism consists of $2$ squares (base and top) and $4$ rectangles (side faces). \n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Surface area} & =2\\times (\\text{Area of the base})+4\\times (\\text{Area of the side}) \\\\  & =2\\times 4+4\\times 6 \\\\  & =8+24 \\\\  & =32\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392817 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1233be4b-7634-5882-9ae5-3200a9da71d8" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Surface Area of a Polyhedron Given the Areas of the Faces: Word Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c6756b1-9c04-53c2-8988-fd64ed444bc1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "154\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392819} {:answer/id #uuid "7246bc39-a4f1-5b94-ba1f-daa3ed6f35e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "172\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392820} {:answer/id #uuid "71f3b47c-ea13-5d62-a2a5-da4411c368b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "124\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392821} {:answer/id #uuid "a9dabb67-2280-5ff5-83a3-c11aa82bd237" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "148\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392822} {:answer/id #uuid "ba34adbb-d578-5bbf-8efa-797cfd602cd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "192\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392823}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ba34adbb-d578-5bbf-8efa-797cfd602cd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "192\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392823} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d067699-acd4-5aa2-807d-ab608abdb9a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392818}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7ddd59ad-58ff-5804-a895-8e6dabf22244" :question/math-academy-id "q-62069" :question/problem "![](images/b9/b9668f2d9988488c64e2bb7edb55ae94b1b84b947e249fbc96c506b1c5095d4d.png)\n\nA manufacturer produces a triangular prism-shaped box to hold chocolate candies. In the figure, the areas of some of the faces are displayed in a net of the prism. What is the total surface area of the box?" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "The surface area of our prism consists of $2$ triangles (base and top) and $3$ rectangles (central and side faces). \n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Surface area} & =2\\times (\\text{Area of the base})+2\\times (\\text{Area of the side faces}) \\\\  & +1\\times (\\text{Area of the central face}) \\\\  & =2\\times 24+2\\times 45+54 \\\\  & =48+90+54 \\\\  & =192\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392824 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "947eb2bb-0443-5740-9d45-847c70877270" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Surface Area of a Polyhedron Given Some Lengths"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b7d05a27-bc22-599f-9521-686cccb47526" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392826} {:answer/id #uuid "b463f199-15d4-5666-95e5-3d376c7e7264" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392827} {:answer/id #uuid "94740290-e368-5fc3-b155-957695497127" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392828} {:answer/id #uuid "dda83309-31a3-50ae-8a59-56d809fefee1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392829} {:answer/id #uuid "950ae5a7-059d-5882-b91e-7d6c0833c44d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392830}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dda83309-31a3-50ae-8a59-56d809fefee1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392829} :answer-field/id #uuid "56ae08df-7b65-5143-b632-e7f82228c84e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392825}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "17e99f21-4a32-5f31-9b6f-4f7bf7f5a533" :question/math-academy-id "q-62107" :question/problem "![](images/24/248ccfcbb3de8ab63622cfaf4c823ae1c1734742f5f8c9f14b1dfae0ff79eed2.png)\n\nWhat is the surface area of the box shown above knowing that  it is $4\\,\\text{m}$ long, $1\\,\\text{m}$ wide, and $1\\,\\text{m}$ tall?" :question/worked-solution "First, let's consider the net of our box.\n\n \n\n![](images/c3/c35bb0f7b049baa569a06532b516436afaf310e28870cfbd4790467d36e54809.png)\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n \n\nFrom the diagram, we have squares and rectangles.\n\nThe rectangles are $4\\,\\text{m}$ long and $1\\,\\text{m}$ high. So, the area of one rectangle is:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area of the rectangle}=4\\times 1=4\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe squares has the side equal to $1\\,\\text{m}.$ Hence, the area of one square is:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area of the square}=1\\times 1=1\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the surface area of the box consists of $4$ rectangles and $2$ squares. \n\nTherefore, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Surface area} & =4\\times (\\text{Area of the rectangle})+2\\times (\\text{Area of the square}) \\\\  & =4\\times 4+2\\times 1 \\\\  & =16+2 \\\\  & =18\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147980 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "947eb2bb-0443-5740-9d45-847c70877270" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Surface Area of a Polyhedron Given Some Lengths"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f5eac203-93f5-57be-af67-8a783ae0e862" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392832} {:answer/id #uuid "a641a258-af1f-5702-ad18-89ae178498fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "46\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392833} {:answer/id #uuid "7749a61c-cb07-5313-b6a7-d0b15011e79c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392834} {:answer/id #uuid "9132f443-bda0-529f-ad02-e2d4be29dfab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392835} {:answer/id #uuid "622ce365-406e-57b1-ab9a-397d4b72cb7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392836}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9132f443-bda0-529f-ad02-e2d4be29dfab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392835} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f220997-6704-5639-ac49-71df6da3aa3c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392831}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8ce742b5-57f4-53b1-882f-51d5011e4a65" :question/math-academy-id "q-62256" :question/problem "![](images/54/54212c293853632fd46716b6216a74c3579bf89349e8b2c5cefafe80e40e42cf.png)\n\nAssuming that all side faces have the same height of $9\\,\\text{m}$, find the total surface area of the square pyramid above using its net." :question/worked-solution "The net of our pyramid is the following.\n\n![](images/09/09eff98ca31b4bb350e2d793dcfa6e6972de25bb3b613c898533068c36fe9a78.png)\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the base of the pyramid is a square with a side equal to $2\\,\\text{m}.$ Hence:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area of the square}=2\\times 2=4\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nEach side face of our solid is triangle, where the base is $2\\,\\text{m}$ and the height is $9\\,\\text{m}.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area of the triangle}=\\frac{1}{2}\\times 2\\times 9=9\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the surface area of the pyramid consists of $1$ square (base) and $4$ triangles (side faces). \n\nTherefore, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Surface area} & =(\\text{Area of the square})+4\\times (\\text{Area of the triangle}) \\\\  & =4+4\\times 9 \\\\  & =4+36 \\\\  & =40\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088229 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "41bccbd5-3d0c-5f00-b506-97b8f83c5472" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order Inhomogeneous ODE with Quadratic Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81cc546e-6ee9-581c-a55e-9944c5ff8e2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{p} =-x^{2} + 2x + 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085999} {:answer/id #uuid "bf6efea9-2618-568a-8b66-4b1178d563cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{p} = 2x^{2} - x + 6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086000} {:answer/id #uuid "1e978853-3633-5fe0-98f4-32dfa6beba5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{p} =-x^{2} + 3x - 6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086001} {:answer/id #uuid "24085b70-9d5a-5784-8a9c-0af91c78a5fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{p} = x^{2} + x + 6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086002} {:answer/id #uuid "7da978a5-3be4-5434-988a-7dd5d8829a4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{p} = x^{2} - 3x - 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086003}] :answer-field/id #uuid "9dac09d1-080f-56f1-a3f4-362ae1f4d608" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085998}] :question/id #uuid "43efe1d0-c638-55a1-8bd9-f43f26e2bc24" :question/math-academy-id "q-64206" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n$\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} + 4\\frac{dy}{dx} - 12y = 12x^{2} - 32x - 30$\nhas the complementary function $y_{c}(x) = Ae^{2x} + Be^{-6x}$, find the particular solution $y_{p}(x)$ to this equation."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148130 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6ed6eeb5-a646-5f47-b6d8-f1078f550376" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Factors Giving an Exact ODE"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "987105b6-6efc-548a-92a2-82bd1f6712c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146505} {:answer/id #uuid "a8d86731-7aed-5f2b-96a0-f421bf69d7d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146506} {:answer/id #uuid "b1e4213a-c93e-5899-bc48-e492bfbcbf17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146507} {:answer/id #uuid "376cc1ab-793a-5c84-81c4-714df491f3c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146508} {:answer/id #uuid "54f9aa97-dc86-50c1-8691-697c9bb46a2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146509}] :answer-field/id #uuid "f83f97ce-cbc9-58c2-bc53-a272b5b0ea58" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146504}] :question/id #uuid "3c92b66b-ccec-5e6d-8f75-9e3b9cdb1f7e" :question/math-academy-id "q-64207" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation:\n\n$P(x, y)dx + 4y^{3}dy = 0$\n\nGiven that the differential equation is exact, which of the following functions could be $P(x, y)$?\n\n1. $\\cos (x) + 8$\n2. $\\ln (x + 1) - 2$\n3. $2x + 7$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088232 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c01e7705-e8a6-5712-ad9a-2fc77ee3cdec" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order ODE with Exponential Forcing and Repeated Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a213e12a-665d-5422-bc38-200399893470" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{p} = xe^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086017} {:answer/id #uuid "a91bad73-2565-58b4-b631-89c0506d7dd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{p} = 4xe^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086018} {:answer/id #uuid "0ca68d42-4cc3-584d-a74a-a4e958c801ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{p} = 2e^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086019} {:answer/id #uuid "6313def7-9201-56e7-97e6-4f60598601ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{p} = 2x^{2}e^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086020} {:answer/id #uuid "77502cd8-b276-58b1-94cc-f04cdfbb6a76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{p} = 5x^{2}e^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086021}] :answer-field/id #uuid "bd4cf951-f24a-5d63-8981-23f9d1f5ef35" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086016}] :question/id #uuid "c49ef0d7-77b7-5469-92f2-ccc1a74380b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-64236" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n$\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} - 2\\frac{dy}{dx} + y = 10e^{x}$\nhas the complementary function $y_{c}(x) = Ae^{x} + Bxe^{x}$, find the particular solution $y_{p}(x)$ to this equation."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148143 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2dedcee-452d-50ff-844e-5648be3066b4" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Amplitude of an Simple Harmonic Oscillation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d7fe1e7-05fa-5407-906a-6234e021a8d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146583} {:answer/id #uuid "b60c7201-6a6a-53d4-a03f-262c14513316" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146584} {:answer/id #uuid "d49102b9-9608-550d-a7ea-f0656947d2a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146585} {:answer/id #uuid "ea9e440f-ac3a-5d3e-921a-352386e6de10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146586} {:answer/id #uuid "3df3185f-2921-5c98-9fe5-09c7c28e3d8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146587}] :answer-field/id #uuid "5c1b78e0-b695-5680-93c7-66bdc6619adb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146582}] :question/id #uuid "012482b3-0bd8-57c6-8936-9b9b3da520e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-64254" :question/problem "The position $y(t)$, measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with simple harmonic motion, is governed by the equation\n\n$y^{″} + 144y = 0$,\n\nwhere $t > 0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with a velocity of $8m/s$, find the amplitude of the oscillation."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148153 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb4b6a5f-9aed-5dad-9356-4038bf9c0acf" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Inverse Laplace Transform Using Partial Fractions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "94649198-edb3-5008-8a29-c00476a960a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "L^{-1}{F(s)} = e^{-2t} - 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146643} {:answer/id #uuid "9bbf9abd-1014-5be1-926c-ca0026659632" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "L^{-1}{F(s)} = 6e^{t} - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146644} {:answer/id #uuid "b9b5daaf-34b0-5358-8310-0f42c6c7b92e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "L^{-1}{F(s)} = e^{2t} + 6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146645} {:answer/id #uuid "3f53451d-7ab7-50bc-836a-12a5bba80b54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "L^{-1}{F(s)} = e^{6t} - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146646} {:answer/id #uuid "5d01a5ff-f3f5-5829-b928-5eaa1b02585c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "L^{-1}{F(s)} = e^{-t} - 6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146647}] :answer-field/id #uuid "5ae6a697-a598-5462-9f9c-da3c43c99d85" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146642}] :question/id #uuid "d006ce4c-8de4-5385-bde9-8b9759c49cd6" :question/math-academy-id "q-64381" :question/problem "Find the inverse Laplace transform for $F(s) = \\frac{6}{s^{2} - 6s}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148161 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7aa5350-6bfc-5057-ad4a-3bd97a31da5e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Series Solution to a Second-Order Initial Value Problem About a Non-Zero Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ffa93ab-030c-52fa-aecf-2a3d5719fe1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=1+2(x-1)+\\frac{5}{6}(x-1)^{3}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392838} {:answer/id #uuid "977f5310-53e6-5c35-99bc-11dc28f39d45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=1+2(x-1)+\\frac{3}{2}(x-1)^{2}+(x-1)^{3}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392839} {:answer/id #uuid "f7176b96-edd3-50a9-aa20-a393741eaa2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=1+2(x-1)^{2}-4(x-1)^{3}⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392840} {:answer/id #uuid "2e964475-323c-56e0-a413-afdced28f3ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=1+2(x-1)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-1)^{2}+3(x-1)^{3}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392841} {:answer/id #uuid "321374a8-9a25-569c-b6ad-bbca19ec4f78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=1+2(x-1)+(x-1)^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}(x-1)^{3}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392842}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "977f5310-53e6-5c35-99bc-11dc28f39d45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=1+2(x-1)+\\frac{3}{2}(x-1)^{2}+(x-1)^{3}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392839} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a427d0e-124f-5b90-8596-c61f0dc3091b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392837}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0ffa6f28-8c99-55f2-8002-5927b3b2b999" :question/math-academy-id "q-64504" :question/problem "Use the Taylor series method to find a series solution about $x=1$, in ascending powers of $(x-1)$ up to and including the term in $(x-1)^{3},$  of the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=xy+{y}^{′},\\,y(1)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=2.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We wish to express $y(x)$ as a Taylor series about $x=1$:\n\n$$\ny(x)=y(1)+(x-1){y}^{′}(1)+\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{2!}{y}^{″}(1)+\\frac{(x-1)^{3}}{3!}{y}^{(3)}(1)+⋯\n$$\n\nWe're given $y(1)$ and ${y}^{′}(1),$ so we need to calculate ${y}^{″}(1)$ and ${y}^{(3)}(1).$\n\nWe substitute $y(1)=1$ and ${y}^{′}(1)=2$ into the given equation to find ${y}^{″}(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}(1) & =1\\cdot y(1)+{y}^{′}(1) \\\\  & =1+2 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, to find an expression for ${y}^{(3)}(x),$ we differentiate both sides on the given equation with respect to $x:$ \n\n$$\n{y}^{(3)}(x)=x{y}^{′}+y+{y}^{″}\n$$\n\nWe can now find  ${y}^{(3)}(1)$ by substituting in the known values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{(3)}(1) & =1\\cdot {y}^{′}(1)+y(1)+{y}^{″}(1) \\\\  & =2+1+3 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we  substitute  the obtained values in  the Taylor series:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & =y(1)+(x-1){y}^{′}(1)+\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{2!}{y}^{″}(1)+\\frac{(x-1)^{3}}{3!}{y}^{(3)}(1)+⋯ \\\\  & =1+(x-1)\\cdot 2+\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{2}\\cdot 3+\\frac{(x-1)^{3}}{6}\\cdot 6+… \\\\  & =1+2(x-1)+\\frac{3}{2}(x-1)^{2}+(x-1)^{3}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392843 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b6663cc0-4aba-5d92-b126-9c09bdac6188" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Series Solution to a Second-Order Initial Value Problem About Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3a867a8-fbef-51ba-83a4-e41532530800" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=-2-3{x}^{2}+\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{3}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392845} {:answer/id #uuid "966dd8bc-e4df-5f54-9eb7-bf292788e15e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=-2-{x}^{2}-\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{3}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392846} {:answer/id #uuid "6da5b2f5-fe75-594a-8b1a-27a054caf1da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=-2+{x}^{2}-\\frac{8{x}^{3}}{3}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392847} {:answer/id #uuid "c8eff9a4-ebf6-57eb-92c9-d9d1ff60099f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=-2-{x}^{2}+\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{3}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392848} {:answer/id #uuid "ff8acf52-1a2a-5533-a95c-a7cb55c606e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=-2+3{x}^{2}+\\frac{8{x}^{3}}{3}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392849}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c8eff9a4-ebf6-57eb-92c9-d9d1ff60099f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=-2-{x}^{2}+\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{3}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392848} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4f7a718-7f3b-562f-869f-422a686ea074" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392844}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3d1d32a0-6249-5ad9-baca-e7fe99dac145" :question/math-academy-id "q-64557" :question/problem "Use the Taylor series method to find a series solution about $x=0$, in ascending powers of $x$ up to and including the term in ${x}^{3},$  of the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=3y+4{e}^{2x},\\,y(0)=-2,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We wish to express $y(x)$ as a Taylor series about $x=0$:\n\n$$\ny(x)=y(0)+x{y}^{′}(0)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}{y}^{″}(0)+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3!}{y}^{(3)}(0)+⋯\n$$\n\nWe're given $y(0)$ and ${y}^{′}(0),$ so we need to calculate ${y}^{″}(0)$ and ${y}^{(3)}(0).$\n\nWe know that $y(0)=-2,$ so we substitute this into the given equation to find ${y}^{″}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}(0) & =3y(0)+4{e}^{2(0)} \\\\  & =3(-2)+4(1) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, to find an expression for ${y}^{(3)}(x),$ we differentiate both sides on the given equation with respect to $x:$ \n\n$$\n{y}^{(3)}=3{y}^{′}+8{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nWe can now find  ${y}^{(3)}(0)$ by substituting in the known values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{(3)}(0) & =3{y}^{′}(0)+8{e}^{2(0)} \\\\  & =3(0)+8 \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinaly, we  substitute  the obtained values in  the Taylor series:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & =y(0)+x{y}^{′}(0)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}{y}^{″}(0)+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3!}{y}^{(3)}(0)+⋯ \\\\  & =-2+x\\cdot 0+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}\\cdot (-2)+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3!}\\cdot 8+⋯ \\\\  & =-2-{x}^{2}+\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{3}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148178 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7aa5350-6bfc-5057-ad4a-3bd97a31da5e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Series Solution to a Second-Order Initial Value Problem About a Non-Zero Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "94c80a08-db20-54a7-8f46-b7cabe2428bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=1+2(x-\\pi )-\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\pi )^{2}-\\frac{1}{12}(x-\\pi )^{3}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392851} {:answer/id #uuid "b0e307b7-4ce5-5e34-9577-e9af06bcb933" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=1+2(x-\\pi )-\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\pi )^{2}+\\frac{1}{6}(x-\\pi )^{3}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392852} {:answer/id #uuid "ecabcbf6-49ae-5c58-93d6-20972aba0520" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=1+2(x-\\pi )-\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\pi )^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}(x-\\pi )^{3}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392853} {:answer/id #uuid "13c60a16-383b-51e0-93ca-232a1ed5d52f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=1+2(x-\\pi )+\\frac{1}{4}(x-\\pi )^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}(x-\\pi )^{3}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392854} {:answer/id #uuid "9b0dbebf-b51e-53ba-9c91-3922b4885afa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=1+2(x-\\pi )+\\frac{1}{4}(x-\\pi )^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\pi )^{3}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392855}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ecabcbf6-49ae-5c58-93d6-20972aba0520" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=1+2(x-\\pi )-\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\pi )^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}(x-\\pi )^{3}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392853} :answer-field/id #uuid "9bc793e3-f6c9-5525-975b-8c5edb275ef5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392850}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4b0a8f90-3a88-582b-9f4a-0fbf1daa3eb9" :question/math-academy-id "q-64561" :question/problem "Use the Taylor series method to find a series solution about $x=\\pi $, in ascending powers of $(x-\\pi )$ up to and including the term in $(x-\\pi )^{3},$  of the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,y(\\pi )=1,\\,{y}^{′}(\\pi )=2.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We wish to express $y(x)$ as a Taylor series about $x=\\pi $:\n\n$$\ny(x)=y(\\pi )+(x-\\pi ){y}^{′}(\\pi )+\\frac{(x-\\pi )^{2}}{2!}{y}^{″}(\\pi )+\\frac{(x-\\pi )^{3}}{3!}{y}^{(3)}(\\pi )+⋯\n$$\n\nWe're given $y(\\pi )$ and ${y}^{′}(\\pi ),$ so we need to calculate ${y}^{″}(\\pi )$ and ${y}^{(3)}(\\pi ).$\n\nWe substitute $y(\\pi )=1$ and ${y}^{′}(\\pi )=2$ into the given equation to find ${y}^{″}(\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}(\\pi ) & =y(\\pi )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, to find an expression for ${y}^{(3)}(x),$ we differentiate both sides on the given equation with respect to $x:$ \n\n$$\n{y}^{(3)}(x)={y}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\nWe can now find  ${y}^{(3)}(\\pi )$ by substituting in the known values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{(3)}(\\pi ) & ={y}^{′}(\\pi )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )-y(\\pi )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =(2\\cdot (-1))-(1\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we  substitute  the obtained values in  the Taylor series:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & =y(\\pi )+(x-\\pi ){y}^{′}(\\pi )+\\frac{(x-\\pi )^{2}}{2!}{y}^{″}(\\pi )+\\frac{(x-\\pi )^{3}}{3!}{y}^{(3)}(\\pi )+⋯ \\\\  & =1+(x-\\pi )\\cdot 2+\\frac{(x-\\pi )^{2}}{2}\\cdot (-1)+\\frac{(x-\\pi )^{3}}{6}\\cdot (-2)+… \\\\  & =1+2(x-\\pi )-\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\pi )^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}(x-\\pi )^{3}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392856 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7aa5350-6bfc-5057-ad4a-3bd97a31da5e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Series Solution to a Second-Order Initial Value Problem About a Non-Zero Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2635e0c2-2ef2-5541-a134-f488c502d86b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=-1-\\frac{1}{2}(x-1)^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}(x-1)^{3}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392858} {:answer/id #uuid "572f9e61-6385-52e2-a91c-69f0d8c1df79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=-1-\\frac{1}{2}(x-1)^{2}+\\frac{1}{6}(x-1)^{3}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392859} {:answer/id #uuid "72979643-3006-566f-8fc1-af7c73f82cc1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=-1-\\frac{1}{2}(x-1)^{2}+2(x-1)^{3}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392860} {:answer/id #uuid "cd2d6ef7-375c-5ff0-9df7-7c2a428e5ad1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=-1+\\frac{3}{2}(x-1)^{2}-2(x-1)^{3}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392861} {:answer/id #uuid "1f77878b-91d5-57a4-8851-28844fc61321" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=-1+\\frac{3}{2}(x-1)^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}(x-1)^{3}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392862}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "572f9e61-6385-52e2-a91c-69f0d8c1df79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=-1-\\frac{1}{2}(x-1)^{2}+\\frac{1}{6}(x-1)^{3}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392859} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6a71283-0f72-5bcd-a4ea-33c88732572b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392857}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9b925fd6-63c4-512c-989c-41491a7f83b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-64564" :question/problem "Use the Taylor series method to find a series solution about $x=1$, in ascending powers of $(x-1)$ up to and including the term in $(x-1)^{3},$  of the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=y{e}^{1-x},\\,y(1)=-1,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We wish to express $y(x)$ as a Taylor series about $x=1$:\n\n$$\ny(x)=y(1)+(x-1){y}^{′}(1)+\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{2!}{y}^{″}(1)+\\frac{(x-1)^{3}}{3!}{y}^{(3)}(1)+⋯\n$$\n\nWe're given $y(1)$ and ${y}^{′}(1),$ so we need to calculate ${y}^{″}(1)$ and ${y}^{(3)}(1).$\n\nWe substitute $y(1)=-1$ and ${y}^{′}(1)=0$ into the given equation to find ${y}^{″}(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}(1) & =y(1){e}^{1-1} \\\\  & =(-1){e}^{0} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, to find an expression for ${y}^{(3)}(x),$ we differentiate both sides on the given equation with respect to $x:$ \n\n$$\n{y}^{(3)}(x)={y}^{′}{e}^{1-x}-y{e}^{1-x}\n$$\n\nWe can now find  ${y}^{(3)}(1)$ by substituting in the known values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{(3)}(1) & ={y}^{′}(1){e}^{1-1}-y(1){e}^{1-1} \\\\  & =(0\\cdot {e}^{0})-(-1\\cdot {e}^{0}) \\\\  & =0+1 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we  substitute  the obtained values in  the Taylor series:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & =y(1)+(x-1){y}^{′}(1)+\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{2!}{y}^{″}(1)+\\frac{(x-1)^{3}}{3!}{y}^{(3)}(1)+⋯ \\\\  & =-1+(x-1)\\cdot 0+\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{2}\\cdot (-1)+\\frac{(x-1)^{3}}{6}\\cdot 1+… \\\\  & =-1-\\frac{1}{2}(x-1)^{2}+\\frac{1}{6}(x-1)^{3}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392863 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f407cd0-11c1-59c6-a7c5-25e4be37e2d5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Series Solution to a First-Order Initial Value Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5688b562-955c-52e2-a47e-7a591492aa39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=2+2{x}^{2}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392865} {:answer/id #uuid "55719890-f9bc-5d07-a9a2-fd83cb617e16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=2+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392866} {:answer/id #uuid "40655268-e069-5aac-8c4d-445e5042e25a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=2+2{x}^{2}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392867} {:answer/id #uuid "efb9cb04-a8cf-5726-a359-41220c95e87c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=2+{x}^{2}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392868} {:answer/id #uuid "f2520c07-786f-5f78-96c2-4df6c140513a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=2+{x}^{2}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392869}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "efb9cb04-a8cf-5726-a359-41220c95e87c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=2+{x}^{2}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392868} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5212084-eef7-5bc4-9ed0-b74d742ac65b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392864}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "92748b26-448f-5bbd-9fea-a5a5cd40c199" :question/math-academy-id "q-64566" :question/problem "Use the Taylor series method to find a series solution about $x=0$, in ascending powers of $x$ up to and including the term in ${x}^{3},$  of the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\\,y(0)=2.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We wish to express $y(x)$ as a Taylor series about $x=0$:\n\n$$\ny(x)=y(0)+x{y}^{′}(0)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}{y}^{″}(0)+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3!}{y}^{(3)}(0)+⋯\n$$\n\nWe're given $y(0),$ so we need to calculate ${y}^{′}(0),$  ${y}^{″}(0)$ and ${y}^{(3)}(0).$\n\nWe know that $y(0)=2,$ so we substitute this into the given equation to find ${y}^{′}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(0) & ={0}^{2}+y(0)\\operatorname{sin}⁡0=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, to find an expression for ${y}^{″}(x),$ we differentiate both sides on the given equation with respect to $x:$ \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)=2x+{y}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nWe can now find  ${y}^{″}(0)$ by substituting in the known values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}(0) & =2(0)+{y}^{′}(0)\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+y(0)\\operatorname{cos}⁡0 \\\\  & =0+0+(2\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find an expression for ${y}^{(3)}(x),$ we differentiate ${y}^{″}(x)$ with respect to $x:$ \n\n$$\n{y}^{(3)}(x)=2+{y}^{″}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2{y}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\nWe can now find  ${y}^{(3)}(0)$ by substituting in the known values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{(3)}(0) & =2+{y}^{″}(0)\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+2{y}^{′}(0)\\operatorname{cos}⁡0-y(0)\\operatorname{sin}⁡0 \\\\  & =2+(2\\cdot 0)+(2\\cdot 0\\cdot 1)-(2\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we  substitute  the obtained values in  the Taylor series:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & =y(0)+x{y}^{′}(0)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}{y}^{″}(0)+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3!}{y}^{(3)}(0)+⋯ \\\\  & =2+x\\cdot 0+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\cdot 2+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}\\cdot 2+⋯ \\\\  & =2+{x}^{2}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148180 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5266e491-2718-56a9-b5a7-f4a86a8a01c7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Position of a Particle Oscillating With Forced Harmonic Motion at a Moment"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d69d6c7b-6c0f-5833-b358-024e1b2373a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(1)=(\\operatorname{cos}⁡1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡1+3)\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392871} {:answer/id #uuid "141a0a74-e302-523c-9f8c-bad2145b337b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(1)=(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2+1)\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392872} {:answer/id #uuid "bc71a800-3396-5d41-b999-dc1cc70c1cd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(1)=(\\operatorname{cos}⁡1-2)\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392873} {:answer/id #uuid "d183e142-084c-5c40-a40a-3e43c9bd291f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(1)=(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2+3)\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392874} {:answer/id #uuid "0d2b2af7-c2a5-5791-bb97-7ee22ccd83a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(1)=(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡3+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3-1)\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392875}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "141a0a74-e302-523c-9f8c-bad2145b337b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(1)=(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2+1)\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392872} :answer-field/id #uuid "2118b5c2-d188-5b07-bc58-c6425a8b5a57" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392870}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9d1bc0c7-99f9-59d0-919b-05c0a19b6daa" :question/math-academy-id "q-64572" :question/problem "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with forced harmonic motion without damping is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4y=8{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with zero velocity, find the position of the particle after $1$ second." :question/worked-solution "The general solution to the equation of forced harmonic motion without damping is the sum of the complementary function and a particular solution.\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ represents simple harmonic motion and can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=4\\,⟹\\,\\omega =2.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(t).$\n\nThe external force is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {t}^{2}+\\beta t+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $ and $\\gamma $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nSubstituting ${y}_{p}$ into the differential equation and equating coefficients gives $\\alpha =2,\\beta =0,\\gamma =-1.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=2{t}^{2}-1,\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t+2{t}^{2}-1.\n$$\n\nSince the particle is initially at the origin with zero velocity, the initial conditions are $y(0)=0$ and ${y}^{′}(0)=0.$\n\nSolving with these conditions for $A$ and $B$ gives\n\n$$\nA=1,\\,B=0.\n$$\n\nSo our solution is\n\n$$\ny(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+2{t}^{2}-1.\n$$\n\nTo find the position of the particle after one second, we substitute into the equation $t=1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2(1)+2(1)^{2}-1 \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2+1)\\,\\text{m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392876 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "48a64312-22d5-57ed-b83b-254aff2ca870" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Period of a Damped Oscillation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d7c71da-e88e-5ef7-9992-57e3285741a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392878} {:answer/id #uuid "87ab7734-6709-5366-9804-65686fe38f5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi \\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392879} {:answer/id #uuid "bc219892-8db2-59ec-8f0e-74228c0dcdd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\pi \\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392880} {:answer/id #uuid "90aaa559-d83e-58cb-8b19-dca2ca99dcd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{5}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392881} {:answer/id #uuid "af845d3e-6ea7-51b2-9edf-f53410cbddd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392882}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d7c71da-e88e-5ef7-9992-57e3285741a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392878} :answer-field/id #uuid "f29649ee-a16d-56d9-80ba-8cd64f7d549a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392877}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9e801805-48d7-5f7f-a2eb-42e2eeee70b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-64623" :question/problem "The position $y(t)$ of a particle oscillating with light damped harmonic motion is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+8{y}^{′}+52y=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. What is the period of the damped oscillation?" :question/worked-solution "The given equation represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. We are looking for solutions of the form $y={e}^{\\lambda t}.$\n\nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +52=0.\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation can be solved by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +52 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +16)-16+52 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +4)^{2}+36 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +4)^{2} & =-36 \\\\ \\lambda +4 & =\\pm 6\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-4\\pm 6\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}={e}^{-4t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡6t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡6t).\n$$\n\nFrom the solution, the value of the angular frequency is $Ω=6\\,{\\text{s}}^{-1}.$\n\nThe period of the damped oscillation $T$ is related to the angular frequency as follows:\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{2\\pi }{Ω}\n$$\n\nSo the period is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{2\\pi }{6}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\,\\text{s}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392883 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dfd8982d-d859-5eb2-9f35-69af39c04822" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching Level Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c5081b5a-0a05-566f-ac65-b452778b3dff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a8/a8c4e910e3884ee68518752ab814f491edb14d422b4c25926b7d3ad34720592d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392885} {:answer/id #uuid "1048cbeb-b7a7-5613-93ec-38f4f470c459" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f6/f6fcb4286cb1d3a0c850e1a5280558ed793b45766359fe796d37e9ff92110300.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392886} {:answer/id #uuid "7aa3bf7c-ba72-5347-9c51-9a74af107731" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f3/f33a74f9c61703628cd53576dc12b8a02d67944dccd70e3ea6d1be88ba3fa5d8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392887} {:answer/id #uuid "36e0d7fc-bba4-5076-b14a-e8abbe7e10db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/2c/2c5dda31d349fb26df687cbe59af5ac163380ae7359561c6809737149f4772a3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392888} {:answer/id #uuid "73047cd2-2772-59bd-ba1e-8f58e56dc0d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/40/408a028814088bbb85e20e52e9482a0ddfd0b21fcf70470196049c6b5f37a208.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392889}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5081b5a-0a05-566f-ac65-b452778b3dff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a8/a8c4e910e3884ee68518752ab814f491edb14d422b4c25926b7d3ad34720592d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392885} :answer-field/id #uuid "a113a983-a1ee-554f-9f6d-a9c10a34e3bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392884}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c2b2fff3-d401-5946-82c7-5c2974317dd2" :question/math-academy-id "q-64717" :question/problem "![](images/36/36bbd69dec59657a74992dbddced8b87190f953687e0d84aa8f3081e8754789c.png)\n\nWhich of the following could represent the level curves of the surface $z=\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ shown above?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the range of $f(x,y)$ is $[-1,1].$ So, we will consider only $c$-level curves for $c\\in [-1,1].$\n\nThe level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equations \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=c\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡y & =c \\\\ y & =\\pm \\operatorname{arccos}⁡c+2\\pi n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ can be any integer. \n\nNotice that in the equations $y=\\pm \\operatorname{arccos}⁡c+2\\pi n$ do not depend on $x.$ So, for a given $c\\in [-1,1]$ and integer $n$, the right-hand side of the equation is constant. As a result, we obtain a family of horizontal lines, as shown below.\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n![](images/a8/a8c4e910e3884ee68518752ab814f491edb14d422b4c25926b7d3ad34720592d.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392890 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4fc0992a-423e-5023-98d5-9d53c3b77788" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral Expression For the Arc Length of a Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "917fe269-7275-5dbe-8043-5ab73f39f23b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{2{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392892} {:answer/id #uuid "c302b461-16a8-5a13-a604-48e2a5a05708" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{3-{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392893} {:answer/id #uuid "610e92dd-f0e2-5bea-8bb6-5a4d0d844cb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{2+{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392894} {:answer/id #uuid "6f4b7c1f-6178-5bb3-8bfd-e481d21ad116" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{2+{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392895} {:answer/id #uuid "c13bb008-eeba-55b4-aec7-73942951b937" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392896}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c13bb008-eeba-55b4-aec7-73942951b937" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392896} :answer-field/id #uuid "e342550d-8ba6-5a84-9cad-9202991768dc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392891}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "719d5fba-71fa-5184-acdb-743568f97311" :question/math-academy-id "q-64723" :question/problem "![](images/d4/d42e9c6ed71f06c2dec273a91d3a4cfbdf5c58095be9370285d7987d38f7b5a6.png)\n\nWhich integral  expression gives the arc length of the curve $h(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+t\\,k$ from  $t=0$ to $t=2\\pi ?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find ${h}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{h}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,k \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+1\\,k \\\\ ‖{h}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }‖{h}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392897 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4fc0992a-423e-5023-98d5-9d53c3b77788" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral Expression For the Arc Length of a Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b393a27d-b443-5b97-b762-ed6603aad1e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{2}^{5}\\sqrt{16{t}^{6}+9{t}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392899} {:answer/id #uuid "66fa210a-e578-5c4d-8132-eb89718f32cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{2}^{5}\\sqrt{{t}^{6}+{t}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392900} {:answer/id #uuid "85f0edfc-dff0-5ee7-a4b2-955898d40c67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{2}^{5}\\sqrt{8{t}^{5}+9{t}^{3}+6}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392901} {:answer/id #uuid "d8531aa7-d3a8-5834-8493-ee803adaa77f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{5}\\sqrt{16{t}^{6}+9}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392902} {:answer/id #uuid "d88974de-cee3-50ec-af8b-a8554a2c05b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{2}^{5}\\sqrt{16{t}^{6}+9{t}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392903}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b393a27d-b443-5b97-b762-ed6603aad1e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{2}^{5}\\sqrt{16{t}^{6}+9{t}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392899} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e4f8290-db9f-5614-851c-76e4decb0150" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392898}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "93e74816-9928-5c75-b857-debb8b7d37d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-64724" :question/problem "Which integral expression gives the arc length of the curve $r(t)={t}^{3}\\,i+3t\\,j+{t}^{4}\\,k$ from  $t=2$ to $t=5?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{4})\\,k \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}\\,i+3\\,j+4{t}^{3}\\,k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(3{t}^{2})^{2}+(3)^{2}+(4{t}^{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9{t}^{4}+9+16{t}^{6}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{2}^{5}‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t={\\int }_{2}^{5}\\sqrt{16{t}^{6}+9{t}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392904 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f6e70249-58d6-57ce-867c-ff9a3b4219f4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Center of the Osculating Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f685ce5-117b-5cb1-93fa-3becdf830cd7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(1,1)$ and the radius is $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392906} {:answer/id #uuid "11c11b67-2a78-5959-ae9c-6b75ceabf943" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(0,0)$ and the radius is $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392907} {:answer/id #uuid "f544673a-81cf-5a3c-8e4e-0f5345a6b56d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(1,0)$ and the radius is $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392908} {:answer/id #uuid "cfb5eb2f-d647-50b3-9ec2-830264b975b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(1,1)$ and the radius is $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392909} {:answer/id #uuid "68cdabe8-d960-54af-a054-681067dc1d73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(0,1)$ and the radius is $\\sqrt{10}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392910}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f685ce5-117b-5cb1-93fa-3becdf830cd7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(1,1)$ and the radius is $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392906} :answer-field/id #uuid "6471e1f5-248e-565f-ace6-4ec44c15f9ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392905}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "42a574c4-fd0d-57cc-b972-6174b753f17e" :question/math-academy-id "q-64836" :question/problem "Find the center and radius of the osculating circle to the curve $r(t)$ at the point $P(3,1)$ where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$, given that $‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})‖=2,$ and the unit tangent vector to the curve is $\\,T(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given both the value of $‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖$ and the unit tangent $T(t)$, we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nNotice that $r(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=⟨3,1⟩,$ since the point $P(3,1)$ is at the tip of $r(\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$ \n\nNow, we find ${T}^{′}(t)$, ${T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and $‖{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩=⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})⟩=⟨-1,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})‖ & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})‖}{‖{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})‖}=\\frac{2}{1}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal vector at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{‖{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})‖}=⟨-1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the center of the osculating circle is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+R(\\frac{\\pi }{2})N(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨3,1⟩+2⟨-1,0⟩=⟨1,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392911 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f9ca4b7-157d-56c9-9302-e7ad08c615c0" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem Involving a Cauchy-Euler Equation with a Repeated Root"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bee072d2-4577-556e-9c87-f8cd21946112" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=2{x}^{5}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392913} {:answer/id #uuid "3b98e7ee-f4b5-55c7-b5b3-42a2da764140" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)={x}^{-5}(7\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392914} {:answer/id #uuid "17d0323e-a430-57f7-8685-c0bb5e6b3131" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)={x}^{-5}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392915} {:answer/id #uuid "7ec65102-49b4-5387-b6bb-bedd5d1879fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=2{x}^{5}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392916} {:answer/id #uuid "ccde75e1-f7c8-5c92-a38b-d5665342b07e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=2{x}^{5}(7\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392917}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ccde75e1-f7c8-5c92-a38b-d5665342b07e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=2{x}^{5}(7\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392917} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d62fa2a-d4d4-5b32-9ed7-a4abdfec29be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392912}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5be694f6-babf-5a30-89e1-81d884ac107b" :question/math-academy-id "q-64883" :question/problem "Find the solution of the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}-9x{y}^{′}+25y=0,\\,y(1)=-2,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=4,\\,x>0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The given equation is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume that the solutions take the form $y={x}^{\\lambda }.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1},\\,{y}^{″}=\\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}-9x\\cdot \\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1}+25{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda }-9\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda }+25{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {x}^{\\lambda }[\\lambda (\\lambda -1)-9\\lambda +25] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda (\\lambda -1)-9\\lambda +25 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda +25 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -5)^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we have a double root $\\lambda =5.$\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)={c}_{1}{x}^{5}+{c}_{2}{x}^{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.\n$$\n\nNow let's find the constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$\n\nSubstituting $y(1)=-2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n-2={c}_{1}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(1)=4.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =5{x}^{4}{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}{x}^{4}+5{c}_{2}{x}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(1)=4$ and ${c}_{1}=-2$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =5{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} \\\\ 4 & =-10+{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2} & =14.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & =-2{x}^{5}+14{x}^{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\  & =2{x}^{5}(7\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392918 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f9ca4b7-157d-56c9-9302-e7ad08c615c0" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem Involving a Cauchy-Euler Equation with a Repeated Root"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c367b6e-fea7-510f-99b8-b52133b254fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)={t}^{6}(4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392920} {:answer/id #uuid "20f374aa-7dba-5a73-8278-84cb5a4bba14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)=4{t}^{6}(2+\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392921} {:answer/id #uuid "f60e006b-f392-517f-8973-8ebb3b0c527f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)=4{t}^{6}(1-6\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392922} {:answer/id #uuid "271b1a20-b2e0-5a35-8330-17a74aa8cce1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)=2{t}^{-6}(1-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392923} {:answer/id #uuid "77a91d39-e494-559a-b713-8c49400438b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)={t}^{-6}(1+6\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392924}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f60e006b-f392-517f-8973-8ebb3b0c527f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)=4{t}^{6}(1-6\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392922} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2ec9bf5-210b-5464-8c5b-59d19bf3af3e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392919}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "92580c12-a8bb-5712-ad7a-3fb0808253a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-64886" :question/problem "Find the solution of the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{t}^{2}{y}^{″}-11t{y}^{′}+36y=0,\\,y(1)=4,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=0,\\,t>0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The given equation is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume that the solutions take the form $y={t}^{\\lambda }.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {t}^{\\lambda -1},\\,{y}^{″}=\\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda -2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda -2}-11t\\cdot \\lambda {t}^{\\lambda -1}+36{t}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda }-11\\lambda {t}^{\\lambda }+36{t}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {t}^{\\lambda }[\\lambda (\\lambda -1)-11\\lambda +36] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda (\\lambda -1)-11\\lambda +36 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-12\\lambda +36 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -6)^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we have a double root $\\lambda =6.$\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny(t)={c}_{1}{t}^{6}+{c}_{2}{t}^{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t.\n$$\n\nNow let's find the constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$\n\nSubstituting $y(1)=4$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n4={c}_{1}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(1)=0.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =6{c}_{1}{t}^{5}+{c}_{2}{t}^{5}+6{c}_{2}{t}^{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(1)=0$ and ${c}_{1}=4$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =6{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} \\\\ 0 & =24+{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2} & =-24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =4{t}^{6}-24{t}^{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t \\\\  & =4{t}^{6}(1-6\\operatorname{ln}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392925 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff16b5e7-d25d-58f1-83d3-e8d4a92d7064" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Cauchy-Euler Equation With Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39965e11-cf0e-54c1-9922-68e12a0fe145" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)={c}_{1}t+{c}_{2}{t}^{3}-\\frac{{t}^{4}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392927} {:answer/id #uuid "77872a95-350b-50f9-9175-1aeacc02ceb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)={c}_{1}t+{c}_{2}{t}^{3}+\\frac{{t}^{4}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392928} {:answer/id #uuid "e62ea67c-8c13-567a-8eb9-44e2f000f311" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)={c}_{1}t+{c}_{2}{t}^{3}+2{t}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392929} {:answer/id #uuid "dc40ebb5-6d7b-5e80-aa34-9bc75a23562f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)={c}_{1}{t}^{-3}+{c}_{2}{t}^{-1}-3{t}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392930} {:answer/id #uuid "8dc46294-c377-5a52-a7db-ef1ee41cfdff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)={c}_{1}{t}^{-3}+{c}_{2}{t}^{-1}+\\frac{3{t}^{4}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392931}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "39965e11-cf0e-54c1-9922-68e12a0fe145" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)={c}_{1}t+{c}_{2}{t}^{3}-\\frac{{t}^{4}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392927} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ed89e50-005a-5575-8aee-a04da4e6edb4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392926}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1e08e670-61ba-56e1-8c2c-f95b70780c3c" :question/math-academy-id "q-64936" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation \n\n$$\n{t}^{2}{y}^{″}-3t{y}^{′}+3y=-{t}^{4},\\,t>0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the complimentary function ${y}_{c}(t)$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n{t}^{2}{y}^{″}-3t{y}^{′}+3y=0.\n$$\n\nLet $y={t}^{\\lambda }.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {t}^{\\lambda -1},\\,{y}^{″}=\\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda -2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda -2}-3t\\cdot \\lambda {t}^{\\lambda -1}+3{t}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda }-3\\lambda {t}^{\\lambda }+3{t}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {t}^{\\lambda }[\\lambda (\\lambda -1)-3\\lambda +3] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda (\\lambda -1)-3\\lambda +3 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +3 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -3)(\\lambda -1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the roots are $\\lambda =1$ and $\\lambda =3.$ Thus, the complimentary function ${y}_{c}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}t+{c}_{2}{t}^{3}.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is proportional to ${t}^{4}.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is of the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\alpha {t}^{4}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is the constant that is to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}t}=4\\alpha {t}^{3},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}=12\\alpha {t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation and group the terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}(12\\alpha {t}^{2})-3t(4\\alpha {t}^{3})+3(\\alpha {t}^{4}) & =-{t}^{4} \\\\ (12\\alpha -12\\alpha +3\\alpha ){t}^{4} & =-{t}^{4} \\\\ 3\\alpha {t}^{4} & =-{t}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get $\\alpha =-\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\nHence, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{{t}^{4}}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & ={y}_{c}(t)+{y}_{p}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}t+{c}_{2}{t}^{3}-\\frac{{t}^{4}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152636 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "85d71b68-d4d4-53c9-8ff7-135389e85edc" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Particular Solution For a Cauchy-Euler Equation Using Variation of Parameters"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4de28c8c-1af9-5a2a-8389-c4f8c4152806" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(t)=-({t}^{2}+t){e}^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392933} {:answer/id #uuid "013d3b52-e8f9-568a-a086-071295b0325b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(t)=({t}^{2}-3t+1){e}^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392934} {:answer/id #uuid "789cbb59-2588-515d-bad2-987f77e44944" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(t)=(2{t}^{2}-2t){e}^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392935} {:answer/id #uuid "8cf5ec1f-2db1-5f3e-b016-164e64604d62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(t)=-3{t}^{2}{e}^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392936} {:answer/id #uuid "bb966fb3-fdb0-5b3e-92e3-c6d4490fb2c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(t)=({t}^{2}+1){e}^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392937}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "789cbb59-2588-515d-bad2-987f77e44944" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(t)=(2{t}^{2}-2t){e}^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392935} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f5ee475-7191-5adc-9894-3114171f5512" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392932}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1c50a075-ab1f-577a-bcdc-c3da8449650f" :question/math-academy-id "q-64976" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n{t}^{2}{y}^{″}-3t{y}^{′}+3y=2{t}^{4}{e}^{t},\\,t>0\n$$\n\nhas the complimentary function ${y}_{c}(t)=At+B{t}^{3}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(t)$ to this equation.\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following result:*\n\n$$\n\\int {t}^{2}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t=({t}^{2}-2t+2){e}^{t}+C\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the differential equation to the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(t){y}^{′}+Q(t)y=f(t).\n$$\n\nSo, we divide both sides of the given equation by ${t}^{2}.$ We get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-\\frac{3}{t}{y}^{′}+\\frac{3}{{t}^{2}}y=2{t}^{2}{e}^{t}.\n$$\n\nWe notice that the forcing term $f(t)=2{t}^{2}{e}^{t}.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions:\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(t)=t,\\,{y}_{2}(t)={t}^{3}\n$$\n\nFirst, let's compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(t)({t}^{3})^{′}-{(t)}^{′}({t}^{3}) \\\\  & =3{t}^{3}-{t}^{3} \\\\  & =2{t}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1}(t)$ and ${y}_{2}(t)$ are independent.\n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, the particular solution of the differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(t) & ={y}_{1}(t)u(t)+{y}_{2}(t)v(t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =-\\int \\frac{f(t){y}_{2}(t)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}t=-\\int {t}^{2}{e}^{t}=-({t}^{2}-2t+2){e}^{t}, \\\\ v(t) & =\\int \\frac{f(t){y}_{1}(t)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}t=\\int {e}^{t}={e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(t) & ={y}_{1}(t)u(t)+{y}_{2}(t)v(t) \\\\  & =-t({t}^{2}-2t+2){e}^{t}+{t}^{3}{e}^{t} \\\\  & =(2{t}^{2}-2t){e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125192 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b15f59e-ef74-5fa2-9af9-448fdcbbbd3d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Possible Pairs of First-Order Partial Derivatives"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab3a2ec4-7d03-5fd9-b43e-71c965fddbc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392939} {:answer/id #uuid "4726f0ca-9602-5e70-8d03-916ad610a1c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392940} {:answer/id #uuid "161b1a80-54db-5e3f-a437-fd14c1174f0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392941} {:answer/id #uuid "990c2915-e8e1-5acf-b51b-9cb9541423a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392942} {:answer/id #uuid "33e1dc34-2d44-5b08-aa60-9beea00a4855" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392943}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab3a2ec4-7d03-5fd9-b43e-71c965fddbc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392939} :answer-field/id #uuid "3c1098e9-a9f8-57a6-bbb4-70d81d7d6df9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392938}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3caf1655-4ce4-5a5f-995b-a06e33069b3a" :question/math-academy-id "q-65127" :question/problem "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Which of the following pairs could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y)?$\n\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)={x}^{3}y-x$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=x{y}^{3}-y$\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)={e}^{x+y}-2y$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)={e}^{x+y}-2x$\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)=\\sqrt{x-1}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=-3$" :question/worked-solution "Since the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal, that is, ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y).$ Let's check if this condition is true for each of our pairs of first-order partial derivatives.\n\n- For the first pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}y-x)={x}^{3} \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{3}-y)={y}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the first pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the second pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x+y}-2y)={e}^{x+y}-2 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x+y}-2x)={e}^{x+y}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the second pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the third pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{x-1})=0 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-3)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$,  the third pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II and III only'."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125195 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4516cb28-0fc0-5c15-b9b2-747c3827e189" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Normal Vector to a Level Surface"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c107a7a-da72-581b-9af1-093e85def0eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3i + 4j + 2k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123741} {:answer/id #uuid "8e687967-5121-5f2b-81e8-878e990bd999" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3i - 2j + 6k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123742} {:answer/id #uuid "999e4e99-7ad1-560b-a8aa-27a054